STORY = {
    7: ("11 June 2010 · Massachusetts and the bitcointalk forum",
        ["In June 2010 almost nobody could buy bitcoin, because no exchange yet served ordinary people. Gavin Andresen, a software developer in Massachusetts, decided to put coins into people's hands.",
         "On 11 June 2010 he announced on the bitcointalk forum a website giving five bitcoins to every visitor who solved a CAPTCHA, a puzzle that shows a human is asking.",
         "He had stocked the site with 1,100 of his own bitcoins, enough for 220 visitors at five coins each, and requests were served first come, first served.",
         "Thousands of people received their first bitcoin this way. When Satoshi Nakamoto withdrew in 2011, Andresen went on to lead the project."],
        "Before bitcoin had a price, giving it away was how it reached its first users."),
    27: ("31 October 2008 · the metzdowd.com cryptography mailing list",
         ["On 31 October 2008 an author writing as Satoshi Nakamoto posted a nine-page paper to the readers of the metzdowd.com cryptography mailing list. The PDF now lives on bitcoin.org.",
          "Section 11 does something unusual for a systems paper: it computes the attacker's odds. It models the race between the honest chain and a secret one as a random walk.",
          "Its table gives the chance that an attacker holding a share q of the hash power catches up, showing that waiting for confirmations makes a double spend unlikely.",
          "The next slide reruns the paper's own procedure and agrees with its table to seven decimal places."],
         "A confirmation is a probability statement, and you can compute it."),
    31: ("15 August 2010 · block 74,638 · the bitcointalk forum",
         ["On 15 August 2010 block 74,638 contained a transaction that paid out about 184 billion bitcoin, thousands of times the 21 million limit. Nobody knows who sent it.",
          "Two of its outputs were so large that, added as 64-bit numbers, they wrapped around to a small number, and the check that outputs do not exceed inputs passed.",
          "Jeff Garzik spotted the strange block and raised it on the forum within the hour. Satoshi Nakamoto shipped a corrected version, 0.3.10, about five hours after the block.",
          "The honest chain, built without the forged coins, overtook the bad one by block 74,691, and the 184 billion bitcoin ceased to exist."],
         "Every node checks every rule, so the missing rule was repaired within hours."),
}

